#!/usr/bin/env bpftrace
/*
 * SESHAT payload-copy probe (P1.1) — counts the user<->kernel payload copies
 * attributed to the SCG gateway PID(s) over a run window, so "memory copies per
 * message" can be reported (a benchmark_features metric). Dividing the copy
 * count by the messages delivered shows directly that the zero-copy paths
 * (routing/kTLS splice) approach ~0 user copies per message while userspace TLS
 * copies each payload in and back out.
 *
 * This script attaches ONLY the copy kprobes. `_copy_to_user` / `_copy_from_user`
 * are inlined or kprobe-blacklisted on some kernels (bpftrace then reports
 * "No matches for kprobe _copy_to_user"), and bpftrace aborts a whole script when
 * ANY single probe fails to attach. The payload-moving *syscall* counters
 * therefore live in the sibling script `mem_syscalls.bt`, run as an independent
 * bpftrace job, so a kernel without attachable copy kprobes still yields the
 * syscall counts (the io_uring-vs-poll/splice differentiators) instead of losing
 * every counter. On such kernels the copy counters simply come back empty and the
 * harness records the run as having no memory-copy data rather than failing.
 *
 * The harness substitutes the `__PID_FILTER__` token with a predicate such as
 * `pid == 1234 || pid == 5678` before running. To use this standalone, replace
 * it yourself, e.g.:
 *     sudo bpftrace -e "$(sed 's/__PID_FILTER__/pid == 1234/' mem_copies.bt)"
 *
 * Requires root (or CAP_BPF + CAP_PERFMON).
 */
kprobe:_copy_to_user   /__PID_FILTER__/ { @copy_to_user = count(); }
kprobe:_copy_from_user /__PID_FILTER__/ { @copy_from_user = count(); }
